1
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:11,440
And that, little lady, is pi to a thousand places.

2
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:13,610
I'd say I'm sorry I asked,

3
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:15,400
except I didn't.

4
00:00:16,030 --> 00:00:18,810
Oh, look, it's the Scientific American

5
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:20,770
that covered the paper Leonard and I wrote.

6
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:22,320
Oh, let me see.

7
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:25,570
I have mixed feelings about doing interviews.

8
00:00:25,570 --> 00:00:27,510
I like the part where I talk.

9
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:30,610
I do not like the part where the other man talks.

10
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:36,110
Sheldon, this article doesn't mention Leonard at all.

11
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:37,650
Well, that can't be right.

12
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:40,580
It only refers to "Dr. Cooper and his team."

13
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:42,170
Did you even talk about him?

14
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:44,700
All I did was answer a few questions about the theory.

15
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,140
And then expressed my gratitude that Scientific American

16
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:50,400
doesn't include any of those smelly perfume cards.

17
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:52,790
Poor Leonard.

18
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,870
Why? The theory he came up with

19
00:00:55,870 --> 00:00:57,440
just got mentioned in Scientific American.

20
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:58,520
He ought to be thrilled.

21
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,230
He might not be.

22
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:02,010
Oh, maybe you're right.

23
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:04,250
He is kind of a lump.

24
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:05,750
Think about it.

25
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:07,290
How would you feel if you were referred to

26
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:09,180
as part of Leonard's team?

27
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:11,280
Oh, I'd be incensed.

28
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,460
So you see what I'm getting at?

29
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,600
Squeaky wheel gets the grease?

30
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:17,630
No.

31
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:20,460
Grass is always greener?

32
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:21,320
Try again.

33
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:23,170
Well, I don't know-- we're all Groot?

34
00:01:23,170 --> 00:01:25,100
Just tell me.

35
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:28,230
Leonard is as much a part of this paper as you are,

36
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:29,820
and he was overlooked.

37
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:31,740
He's going to feel bad.

38
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:33,510
But it wasn't my fault.

39
00:01:33,510 --> 00:01:35,050
I didn't exclude him.

40
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:37,130
And I didn't write the article.

41
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:39,510
Remember that time you didn't get picked to

42
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:41,490
pull the sword out of the stone at Disneyland

43
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:42,970
and they let that other kid do it?

44
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:45,320
Oh, that kid!

45
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:51,180
Poor Leonard.

46
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:53,200
Exactly.

47
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,550
For the record, that kid was a terrible choice.

48
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:57,630
If you cry when you drop your churro,

49
00:01:57,630 --> 00:02:00,360
you do not have what it takes to rule England.

50
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:30,870
Thanks for helping us sort through all my mom's stuff.

51
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:32,170
Of course.

52
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:33,320
I know what it's like

53
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,880
having to go through a loved one's possessions.

54
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:38,700
My uncle was a worshipper of Krishna.

55
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:41,150
But after he died, you know what we found?

56
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,810
A statue of Shiva.

57
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:48,510
It may not be Crips and Bloods, okay?

58
00:02:48,510 --> 00:02:50,930
But in India, it's a thing.

59
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:53,990
Well, it'll be good that you're there.

60
00:02:53,990 --> 00:02:55,260
Howard's been having trouble deciding

61
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,380
what to keep and what to let go.

62
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:58,660
Well, it's hard.

63
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:02,070
A lot of Ma's stuff brings back fond memories.

64
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:03,080
Is that why you couldn't get rid of

65
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,970
her drawer full of ketchup packets?

66
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:07,280
Yes.

67
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:09,780
It reminds me of us eating out and her saying,

68
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:13,100
"Quick, nobody's looking, fill your pockets with ketchup."

69
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:21,520
Leonard?

70
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:23,830
Have you ever noticed

71
00:03:23,830 --> 00:03:28,580
that only my name is on the cable bill?

72
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,290
Yeah.

73
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:33,800
Good, good.

74
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,510
And your name is on the electric bill

75
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:37,470
and mine isn't.

76
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:39,180
And I'm okay with that.

77
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,580
Actually, your name is on the electric bill.

78
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:43,360
Oh, right.

79
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,490
This is a disaster.

80
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,380
I don't even know what you're talking about and I agree.

81
00:03:52,210 --> 00:03:55,720
Okay, I have to tell you something,

82
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,560
but you're not going to like it.

83
00:03:57,560 --> 00:03:59,010
What's going on?

84
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:01,600
An article was just published

85
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:04,760
in Scientific American about our theory.

86
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,170
Did they hate it?

87
00:04:06,170 --> 00:04:08,110
No. No, they loved it.

88
00:04:08,110 --> 00:04:11,060
They, uh, couldn't say enough nice things about it.

89
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,430
So what's the problem?

90
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:17,120
You know how the PennySaver only has my name...

91
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:18,350
Sheldon!

92
00:04:19,810 --> 00:04:21,860
They didn't mention you in the article.

93
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,370
Only me.

94
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:25,100
Really?

95
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:26,080
I know.

96
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,040
It's not fair.

97
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:33,430
Let the anger go, buddy.

98
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:34,510
You just...

99
00:04:34,510 --> 00:04:37,100
you relax all your muscles.

100
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:39,790
Except for your pubococcygeus and anal sphincter.

101
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:41,990
Let's keep those tight.

102
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:45,610
Uh, that-that's not necessary.

103
00:04:45,610 --> 00:04:49,080
It is-- they're what hold back the urine and the feces.

104
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:51,520
Look, maybe-maybe you shouldn't read it.

105
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:53,680
It'll only make you feel worse.

106
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:55,530
"Cooper and his team"?

107
00:04:55,530 --> 00:04:58,700
You should know I had nothing to do with that.

108
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:02,870
At least they're talking about the theory.

109
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:05,940
I mean, that's what's important.

110
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,760
You know, you're right.

111
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:11,030
Yeah. You know, it's like when Stan Lee and Steve Ditko

112
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:12,530
created Spider-Man.

113
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:14,390
Stan Lee may get all the credit,

114
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:17,350
but Steve Ditko knows he was just as important.

115
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:20,440
Even though Stan Lee gets to be in all the Marvel movies.

116
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,520
And-and he's far richer.

117
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,180
And he's a household name, you know?

118
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:26,290
Whereas, you know, you say Ditko,

119
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:29,980
and that sounds like a company that makes Dits.

120
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,470
That's not helping.

121
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:36,600
Well, I'd give more examples,

122
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:39,940
but, well, everyone in your position's so forgettable.

123
00:05:42,930 --> 00:05:44,890
Stuart, we're here.

124
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:47,640
It's nice of you to let him keep staying at your mom's house.

125
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,210
Yeah, well, I tried putting him out on the curb,

126
00:05:50,210 --> 00:05:52,660
but nobody took him.

127
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:56,790
Just so you know, the power's out.

128
00:05:56,790 --> 00:05:57,700
What happened?

129
00:05:57,740 --> 00:06:00,240
I called, they said a transformer blew.

130
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,220
It's the whole block.

131
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:02,940
Should be fixed by tomorrow.

132
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:04,700
Wait, when did it go out?

133
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:06,360
In the middle of the night.

134
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,160
Oh, my God.

135
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:10,580
Howie, what's wrong?

136
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:15,590
It's all defrosting.

137
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:17,090
It's okay. It's just food.

138
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:20,290
It's not just food.

139
00:06:20,290 --> 00:06:23,350
This is the last food my mother ever made.

140
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:25,430
This is her last brisket.

141
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:27,730
This is her last meatloaf.

142
00:06:27,730 --> 00:06:29,010
This is her last...

143
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:33,300
I have no idea what this is, but...

144
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,190
it's the last one.

145
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:37,480
Everything okay?

146
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:38,870
No.

147
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:41,690
All Ma's food is gonna be ruined.

148
00:06:41,690 --> 00:06:43,950
Well, why don't we take it home and put it in our freezer?

149
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:45,660
You don't want to do that.

150
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,790
Refreezing not only removes moisture,

151
00:06:47,790 --> 00:06:49,900
but it creates ice crystals in the fibers of the meat

152
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:52,860
that can compromise its taste and texture.

153
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:55,570
What? I saw a teachable moment.

154
00:06:55,570 --> 00:06:58,420
I dropped some culinary science.

155
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:01,940
What should we do?

156
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,320
I'll tell you exactly what we're gonna do.

157
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:06,310
We're gonna eat it.

158
00:07:06,310 --> 00:07:08,060
There's, like, 20 pounds of food in there.

159
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:10,630
All you said was I had to get rid of things.

160
00:07:10,630 --> 00:07:14,030
You didn't say they couldn't pass through my colon first.

161
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:16,150
Howie...

162
00:07:17,390 --> 00:07:18,490
Okay.

163
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:21,360
Then how about this?

164
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:23,670
Let's invite everyone over to dinner.

165
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:27,730
It'll be like Ma's feeding us one last time.

166
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:30,760
I love that.

167
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:32,690
Me, too.

168
00:07:33,930 --> 00:07:35,960
Oh, look, we've got enough ketchup

169
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,360
to cover every ounce of it.

170
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:44,030
I mean, who even reads Scientific American?

171
00:07:47,630 --> 00:07:49,400
It's kind of a big deal.

172
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,380
If it's such a big deal, how come the biggest celebrity

173
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:54,200
they could get for the cover is a molecule?

174
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:58,370
Can we please just stop talking about it?

175
00:07:58,370 --> 00:07:59,990
I'm sorry. What can I do?

176
00:07:59,990 --> 00:08:01,960
Nothing, I'm fine.

177
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:04,390
No, no. I'm gonna cheer you up.

178
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:08,540
Here we go.

179
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:09,530
What are we doing?

180
00:08:09,530 --> 00:08:11,230
I am taking you shopping.

181
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:14,870
My baby is sad, and I'm gonna make him happy again.

182
00:08:14,870 --> 00:08:16,590
Look, I know shopping cheers you up,

183
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:18,680
but it's just not really my thing.

184
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:20,330
Well, what about this helicopter

185
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:22,510
you control with an iPad?

186
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:24,240
Does it have a camera in it?

187
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:28,420
It does have a camera in it.

188
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:30,690
Baby's listening.

189
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:33,140
Leonard?

190
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,180
Leonard? Leonard?

191
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:35,930
What?

192
00:08:35,930 --> 00:08:38,640
I spoke to the reporter at the magazine.

193
00:08:40,350 --> 00:08:42,370
What happened? Did you tell him it was my idea?

194
00:08:42,370 --> 00:08:43,570
I did.

195
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:45,550
Thank you. What did he say?

196
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:48,280
He said they made the editorial decision

197
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,540
to only cite the lead scientist.

198
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:52,780
Why did he think you're the lead scientist?

199
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:54,230
It was my idea!

200
00:08:54,230 --> 00:08:55,520
You know, for an extra four bucks,

201
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:57,820
I can have this thing here tomorrow.

202
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:00,540
Well, I know it was your idea,

203
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:03,120
but the reporter said he's been following my work for a while,

204
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:04,960
and the only reason they even mentioned it in the magazine

205
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:06,200
is 'cause my name is on it.

206
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:08,450
You know what? I did it. What's four bucks?

207
00:09:09,270 --> 00:09:11,270
If you're trying to make me feel better,

208
00:09:11,270 --> 00:09:12,690
it's not working.

209
00:09:12,690 --> 00:09:15,350
Well... then what if I told you

210
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:18,340
that I added your name to the cable bill?

211
00:09:21,130 --> 00:09:22,770
No.

212
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:24,650
That's just as well. They had me on hold for 20 minutes

213
00:09:24,650 --> 00:09:26,070
and I hung up. I...

214
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:30,250
Leonard? Leonard?

215
00:09:30,630 --> 00:09:32,070
Leonard?

216
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:38,140
Also, Wolowitz invited us all to dinner, so...

217
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:40,970
I'm ready whenever you are.

218
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:49,740
All he had was an idea.

219
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:51,460
Well, that is an important part.

220
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:52,860
Oh, please!

221
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:54,920
I have ideas all day long.

222
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:59,260
Reverse SeaWorld where dolphins are allowed to pet people.

223
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:04,990
A new clothing size between medium and large called Marge.

224
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:08,090
Snow White retold from the point of view of Sneezy.

225
00:10:08,090 --> 00:10:09,990
Why won't Doc prescribe him something?

226
00:10:09,990 --> 00:10:11,870
We finally find out.

227
00:10:12,770 --> 00:10:15,020
But Leonard's idea was good.

228
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:19,830
Fine. Then Grumpy-- what's he so grumpy about?

229
00:10:19,830 --> 00:10:22,760
Maybe his girlfriend won't take his side.

230
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:26,490
I'm the one who thought of it!

231
00:10:26,490 --> 00:10:28,540
Well, didn't he do a lot of the work?

232
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:30,670
But now he's happy to let people think

233
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:33,040
he's responsible for everything.

234
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:37,050
And that's why you get an iPad helicopter!

235
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:41,750
Is it my fault I have a much bigger reputation than he does?

236
00:10:41,750 --> 00:10:43,020
It's not your fault.

237
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:45,030
Is it my fault that my name came first

238
00:10:45,030 --> 00:10:46,490
on the paper alphabetically?

239
00:10:46,490 --> 00:10:47,460
Not your fault.

240
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:49,310
Is it my fault that when the reporter cited me

241
00:10:49,310 --> 00:10:52,150
as the lead scientist I didn't correct him?

242
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:55,110
Hey, look at that pretty bird.

243
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:57,860
He always needs all the attention.

244
00:10:57,860 --> 00:10:58,950
He's such a baby.

245
00:10:58,950 --> 00:11:00,450
I know, I know.

246
00:11:00,450 --> 00:11:03,890
I swear, he is never ever playing with my helicopter.

247
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:10,050
Okay, I'll start heating some of this stuff up.

248
00:11:10,050 --> 00:11:11,010
Thanks.

249
00:11:11,010 --> 00:11:12,500
Oh, man.

250
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:16,620
This is the boutonniere from my high school prom...

251
00:11:17,410 --> 00:11:20,920
...A piece of cake from my Bar Mitzvah.

252
00:11:21,660 --> 00:11:23,150
Did she throw anything away?

253
00:11:23,150 --> 00:11:24,070
No.

254
00:11:24,070 --> 00:11:27,160
If I find my foreskin, I'm gonna kill myself.

255
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:32,610
Okay, so we've got, um,

256
00:11:32,610 --> 00:11:35,650
three briskets, four meatloafs, one lasagna...

257
00:11:35,650 --> 00:11:37,400
No, that's noodle kugel.

258
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,690
One Jewish lasagna...

259
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:43,640
...two pound cakes that are about eight pounds each,

260
00:11:43,640 --> 00:11:47,460
and one giant container of matzah ball soup.

261
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:50,880
Ma always kept it on hand,

262
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:52,890
in case I got sick.

263
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:58,750
She thought she could cure anything with her cooking.

264
00:11:58,750 --> 00:12:02,520
Even the time I got food poisoning.

265
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:05,370
From her cooking.

266
00:12:08,330 --> 00:12:09,750
You okay?

267
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:12,700
Yeah, I'm okay.

268
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:13,720
Let's get started.

269
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:15,130
You got it.

270
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:24,010
I'm never gonna talk to her again.

271
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:33,270
Should we tell everyone not to come?

272
00:12:33,270 --> 00:12:36,800
No. I want to do this.

273
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:40,230
Okay. Well, I'll keep it together if you can.

274
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:41,710
Okay.

275
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:45,210
I'm not making any promises!

276
00:12:48,710 --> 00:12:50,080
Glad you guys could make it.

277
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:50,900
Of course.

278
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:52,310
Wow, it looks really pretty in here.

279
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:57,070
Yeah, turns out half a dozen menorahs really sets a mood.

280
00:12:58,050 --> 00:12:59,730
So, Stuart, have you thought about what you'd do

281
00:12:59,730 --> 00:13:01,010
if Howard sells the house?

282
00:13:01,010 --> 00:13:03,440
And there goes the mood.

283
00:13:03,990 --> 00:13:05,650
Hi. Hello.

284
00:13:05,650 --> 00:13:07,570
Oh, and a special hello to Leonard,

285
00:13:07,570 --> 00:13:09,870
who needs to be mentioned by name.

286
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:12,740
Subtle.

287
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:14,540
But you got it, right?

288
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:17,260
Hey, guys.

289
00:13:18,150 --> 00:13:19,530
I just want everyone to know,

290
00:13:19,530 --> 00:13:22,680
uh, tonight's not a sad occasion.

291
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:24,140
Yeah, we just want to have the kind of dinner

292
00:13:24,140 --> 00:13:26,290
that we've all had here so many times before.

293
00:13:26,290 --> 00:13:29,160
Good food, good friends, and sometime around midnight,

294
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:31,870
heartburn that makes you pray for death.

295
00:13:33,430 --> 00:13:34,700
Do you need any help in the kitchen?

296
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:37,540
No, we got it. You guys make yourselves comfortable.

297
00:13:38,810 --> 00:13:42,050
All right, hey, you two, we're here for Howard right now, okay?

298
00:13:42,050 --> 00:13:43,990
Yes, so please behave yourselves.

299
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:45,880
- Fine. - Of course.

300
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:51,700
So I heard you two, uh, wrote a paper together.

301
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:53,310
How's that going?

302
00:13:57,060 --> 00:13:58,600
This food is amazing.

303
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:01,000
Mm, and not a vegetable in sight.

304
00:14:01,610 --> 00:14:02,580
That's not true.

305
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:04,650
We've got tomatoes right here.

306
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:10,190
All these bright people sitting around a table by candlelight.

307
00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:13,320
Feels like we could be an 18th century French salon.

308
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:15,070
Indeed.

309
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:17,830
Penny, a salon is a gathering

310
00:14:17,830 --> 00:14:20,000
where intellectuals entertained each other

311
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
with sparkling conversations about issues of the day.

312
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,920
Oh, so it's like The View.

313
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:31,140
Sheldon, The View is a daytime talk show hosted by women.

314
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:33,440
Oh, I'm aware.

315
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,180
It features Whoopi Goldberg.

316
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:37,980
She played Guinan on Star Trek: Next Gen.

317
00:14:38,570 --> 00:14:40,640
Penny, Next Gen refers to

318
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:42,090
- Star Trek... - Shut up.

319
00:14:43,810 --> 00:14:46,610
I would like to propose a salon topic.

320
00:14:46,610 --> 00:14:48,350
Ooh, please do, Rajesh.

321
00:14:48,350 --> 00:14:50,310
The lead in The Hunger Games is a woman.

322
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:52,900
Marvel has made "Tor" a female.

323
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:55,040
Wait, who's Tor?

324
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:58,810
You know, Tor, the God of "Tunder."

325
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:02,280
As I was saying, is this a sign

326
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:06,560
that our society is approaching gender equality?

327
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:09,130
Certainly a lot more women are reading comic books now.

328
00:15:09,130 --> 00:15:10,110
It's true.

329
00:15:10,110 --> 00:15:12,910
At the store, I had to put a seat on the toilet.

330
00:15:14,490 --> 00:15:16,000
We won't know if there's equality

331
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,630
until female Thor has a baby

332
00:15:17,630 --> 00:15:20,850
and the Avengers are cool with her pumping breast milk at work.

333
00:15:21,850 --> 00:15:23,620
I wonder if only a baby who is worthy

334
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:26,410
can suckle at the bosom of Thor.

335
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:28,440
Okay, new salon topic.

336
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:30,860
Salons-- dumb thing from a long time ago

337
00:15:30,860 --> 00:15:34,150
or interesting thing made dumb by talking about superheroes?

338
00:15:34,150 --> 00:15:35,730
Discuss.

339
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:39,110
I don't believe it matters what the topic is.

340
00:15:39,110 --> 00:15:41,050
What's crucial for a salon

341
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:42,590
is that we conduct the discussion

342
00:15:42,590 --> 00:15:45,080
in an elevated and insightful way.

343
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:47,200
It's all about the execution.

344
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:50,760
Of course you'd focus on that rather than the inspiration.

345
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:52,200
Uh, new salon topic.

346
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:55,640
What's more important, an idea or its execution?

347
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:56,700
Oh, that's fun.

348
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:58,230
Yeah, good for you, Leonard.

349
00:15:58,230 --> 00:15:59,910
That's a lovely little notion.

350
00:15:59,910 --> 00:16:01,190
Kind of like,

351
00:16:01,190 --> 00:16:03,790
"I wish I could talk to my uncle in Chicago."

352
00:16:03,790 --> 00:16:06,940
Yeah, now stand back while I invent the telephone.

353
00:16:06,940 --> 00:16:08,770
"Hello? Oh, hold on."

354
00:16:08,770 --> 00:16:09,900
Leonard, it's your uncle.

355
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:12,110
He says you just got burned.

356
00:16:13,510 --> 00:16:14,220
Sheldon.

357
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:14,910
You guys.

358
00:16:14,910 --> 00:16:15,890
No, it's okay.

359
00:16:15,890 --> 00:16:18,740
We're all adults trying to have an intelligent discussion.

360
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:19,540
At least I am.

361
00:16:19,540 --> 00:16:21,170
Howard, what do you think?

362
00:16:21,170 --> 00:16:23,930
Well, I guess, as an engineer,

363
00:16:23,930 --> 00:16:25,930
lean towards execution.

364
00:16:25,930 --> 00:16:27,910
I spend my days trying to take

365
00:16:27,910 --> 00:16:30,120
ideas and make them real.

366
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:32,140
Well said, old chap.

367
00:16:32,140 --> 00:16:34,790
I thought eloquence had died; here it stands before us.

368
00:16:34,790 --> 00:16:36,600
Starting today, it'll go: Gettysburg Address,

369
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,740
"I have a dream" and what you just said.

370
00:16:39,450 --> 00:16:40,960
Oh, now he's a genius?

371
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:43,330
All you ever do is make fun of him and engineering.

372
00:16:43,330 --> 00:16:45,790
Leonard, please. His mother just died.

373
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:48,280
You're being ridiculous.

374
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:49,020
Yeah, so are you.

375
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:50,650
Guys, if I wanted to hear people be bitchy,

376
00:16:50,650 --> 00:16:52,710
I'd go to my real salon.

377
00:16:53,190 --> 00:16:55,050
Leonard, lots of people could've had that idea,

378
00:16:55,050 --> 00:16:57,790
but very few people could've worked out the math the way I did.

379
00:16:57,790 --> 00:17:00,590
Lots of people also didn't have that idea,

380
00:17:00,590 --> 00:17:03,160
like everyone in the entire world except for me.

381
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:05,310
Oh, well, apparently Leonard thinks

382
00:17:05,310 --> 00:17:07,420
he's better than everyone in the whole world,

383
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:09,680
including those fighting for our freedom.

384
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:10,850
Yeah, well, I don't know about you,

385
00:17:10,850 --> 00:17:13,470
but I support our boys overseas.

386
00:17:15,150 --> 00:17:15,980
And girls.

387
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:18,330
Hey, you already ruined Thor; give it a rest.

388
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:21,320
So I'm just supposed to be okay

389
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:23,350
with you hogging all the credit?!

390
00:17:23,350 --> 00:17:25,170
I didn't hog anything.

391
00:17:25,170 --> 00:17:28,890
Unlike you and that weird lasagna with raisins in it.

392
00:17:28,890 --> 00:17:29,710
You want some of this?

393
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:30,480
I'll give you some.

394
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:33,720
Sheldon, Leonard, living room, right now!

395
00:17:39,350 --> 00:17:41,920
She said my name first; that must kill you.

396
00:17:43,750 --> 00:17:45,230
I don't know what you think you're doing,

397
00:17:45,230 --> 00:17:47,740
but this is a very difficult time for my husband!

398
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:50,240
We're eating the last food his mother ever made,

399
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:52,830
and you were gonna throw it at each other like children?!

400
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:55,290
Whatever it is you're fighting about, put it aside,

401
00:17:55,290 --> 00:17:57,240
go back in there and be a good friend to Howard

402
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:00,110
or there's no dessert for either of you!

403
00:18:03,830 --> 00:18:05,370
Look at me when I'm talking to you!

404
00:18:05,370 --> 00:18:05,980
And don't think...

405
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:07,820
You guys ever notice sometimes

406
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:10,340
Bernadette sounds like my mom?

407
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:12,090
I don't hear it.

408
00:18:12,090 --> 00:18:13,440
No, not at all.

409
00:18:25,610 --> 00:18:29,070
I don't think I've eaten that much in my entire life.

410
00:18:29,070 --> 00:18:32,420
It's why my people wandered the desert for 40 years.

411
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:34,580
Took that long to walk it off.

412
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:38,640
You see, Penny, the Israelites were in the desert...

413
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:40,280
Shut up!

414
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:44,380
So glad you two are done fighting.

415
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:48,260
Right now, I'm just trying to burp without throwing up.

416
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:50,360
Hang on.

417
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:52,790
Physics Today mentioned the paper.

418
00:18:52,790 --> 00:18:53,930
What'd they say?

419
00:18:53,930 --> 00:18:56,420
Who cares? Did they mention Leonard?

420
00:18:56,420 --> 00:18:57,750
They did.

421
00:18:57,750 --> 00:18:59,700
Yay.

422
00:19:00,730 --> 00:19:02,630
Good news, I found more Tums.

423
00:19:02,630 --> 00:19:04,850
Yay!

